303 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 103 0 R
/T (deque\(maxlen=7\): append\(x\) automatically discards the oldest value in O\(1\); keep a running sum, so the whole job is O\(n\) time with only O\(7\) = O\(1\) extra window space \(no new list is built per window as slicing does\). Measured 0.078 s, between naive \(0.174 s\) and the list running sum \(0.052 s\) because of Python-level deque handling.)
/E (deque\(maxlen=7\): append\(x\) automatically discards the oldest value in O\(1\); keep a running sum, so the whole job is O\(n\) time with only O\(7\) = O\(1\) extra window space \(no new list is built per window as slicing does\). Measured 0.078 s, between naive \(0.174 s\) and the list running sum \(0.052 s\) because of Python-level deque handling.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
304 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 103 0 R
/T (Dictionary keyed by date: insert/lookup of any date is O\(1\) average, so late readings need no shifting and gaps are natural. The window is 7 lookups per day: O\(7n\), or O\(n\) with a running sum. Space is O\(n\) but larger \(hash table with keys + values, roughly 3-4x a list\).)
