/T (How a dictionary finds a key: it computes hash\(key\), maps it to a slot in a hash table, compares the stored key and \(if another key is there\) probes the next slot. The table is resized to keep it less than about 2/3 full, so the expected number of probes is a constant that does not depend on how many keys are stored: O\(1\) average lookup \(O\(n\) worst case\).)
/E (How a dictionary finds a key: it computes hash\(key\), maps it to a slot in a hash table, compares the stored key and \(if another key is there\) probes the next slot. The table is resized to keep it less than about 2/3 full, so the expected number of probes is a constant that does not depend on how many keys are stored: O\(1\) average lookup \(O\(n\) worst case\).)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [85]
>>
endobj
644 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 108 0 R
/T (Dictionary vs list.count\(\): a dictionary loop touches each of n words once: O\(n\). Calling list.count\(w\) for each of the d distinct words scans the whole list every time: O\(n x d\). Measured with n = 50,000 words: d = 50 gives 0.009 s vs 0.031 s; d = 500 gives 0.005 s vs 0.437 s; d = 5,000 gives 0.006 s vs 3.40 s. The dictionary time stays flat while list.count grows with d.)
/E (Dictionary vs list.count\(\): a dictionary loop touches each of n words once: O\(n\). Calling list.count\(w\) for each of the d distinct words scans the whole list every time: O\(n x d\). Measured with n = 50,000 words: d = 50 gives 0.009 s vs 0.031 s; d = 500 gives 0.005 s vs 0.437 s; d = 5,000 gives 0.006 s vs 3.40 s. The dictionary time stays flat while list.count grows with d.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [86]
>>
endobj
