/Title (Slices \(see demo in output\): readings[7:14] = indices 7-13, i.e. days 8-14 \(the 2nd week, 7 values, end excluded\); readings[-7:] = the last seven readings \(final week\); readings[::7] = every 7th reading from index 0 \(days 1, 8, 15, ...; 53 values for 365 days\).)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1742 0 R
/Next 1919 0 R
>>
endobj
1856 0 obj
<<
/Title (Edge cases: empty list and a list with fewer than 7 values return [] \(no full window exists\); exactly 7 values give one average.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1742 0 R
/Prev 1920 0 R
/First 1921 0 R
/Last 1922 0 R
/Count -2
>>
endobj
1857 0 obj
<<
/Title (deque\(maxlen=7\): append\(x\) automatically discards the oldest value in O\(1\); keep a running sum, so the whole job is O\(n\) time with only O\(7\) = O\(1\) extra window space \(no new list is built per window as slicing does\). Measured 0.078 s, between naive \(0.174 s\) and the list running sum \(0.052 s\) because of Python-level deque handling.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1744 0 R
/Next 1923 0 R
>>
endobj
1858 0 obj
<<
/Title (Summary: slicing a list = O\(7n\) time, O\(n\) data + O\(7\) temporary slice; running sum = O\(n\) time, O\(1\) extra; deque = O\(n\) time, O\(1\) extra; dict = O\(n\) time, higher O\(n\) space but O\(1\) inserts.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1744 0 R
/Prev 1923 0 R
>>
endobj
